<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen, AIMIM, MIM</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen is a political party founded in Hyderabad State in 1927 to promote the interests of Muslims and the Nizam's rule. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasim-razvi">Qasim Razvi</a> in 1946–1948 it opposed accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and organised the Razakar militia. Banned after the Indian annexation of 1948, it was revived in the late 1950s as the All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen and remains active in Hyderabad politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-india-majlis-e-ittehadul-muslimeen.jpg" alt="All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VNC200 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_India_Majlis-e-Ittehadul_Muslimeen_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen (MIM) was founded in 1927 by Nawab Bahadur Yar Jung and Nawab Mahmood Nawaz Khan Qiledar of Hyderabad State; its first meeting was held at Nawaz Khan's house on 12 November 1927. At first it had only a cultural and religious manifesto, but it soon took on a political character. Bahadur Yar Jung was elected its president in 1938 and died in 1944. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the police action known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a>, the Razakars and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasim-razvi">Qasim Razvi</a> were arrested.</p><p>Razvi was released in 1957 on condition that he go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. In 1958, before leaving, he named Abdul Wahid Owaisi, a lawyer, as his successor, and Owaisi organised the party as the All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen. Under him it moved from a hardline policy of independence to a more pragmatic course. His son Sultan Salahuddin Owaisi took control in 1975 and was called Salar-e-Millat, commander of the community. The party's later president, Asaduddin Owaisi, has claimed that AIMIM is not the descendant of the Razakars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party's base was the old city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. It won the Mallepally ward of the Hyderabad Municipal Corporation in 1960, and Salahuddin Owaisi, elected to the state assembly from 1962, won the Hyderabad seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> in 1984 and held it until 2004; the party kept it after him. A split in 1993, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>'s faction formed the Majlis Bachao Tehreek, left AIMIM with a single assembly seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> in 1994. It joined the Congress-led United Progressive Alliance in 2008 and left it in 2012. In the 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> elections it won seven seats and was recognised as a state party.</p><p>In more recent years the party spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. Imtiyaz Jaleel won the Aurangabad Lok Sabha seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> in 2019, the party's first outside Hyderabad, and lost it in 2024. In Bihar AIMIM won five assembly seats in the Seemanchal region in 2020; four of those members joined the RJD on 29 June 2022, and the party again won five seats in 2025. It also won municipal seats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. The party has at times fielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> candidates, and three of Hyderabad's mayors elected from its ranks, among them K. Prakash Rao, were Hindus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “All India Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen</b> under Qasim Razvi organised the Razakars, a paramilitary force that attacked opponents of the regime and Hindu villages, while the State Congress campaigned for accession and the Communist Party led a peasant insurrection in Telangana against the landlords and the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
